### Photon Background Correction

The \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\) sample will contain contributions from multiple sources dominated by asymmetric \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) decays. This contribution is removed at the level of event-averaged distributions via a statistical subtraction utilizing a data-driven estimation of the background present as a function of the trigger's transverse energy [176].

The estimation relies on comparing the NS per-trigger yields of TPC tracks between \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\) triggers. The following calculation makes two major assumptions:

**Assumption 1,**: that \(\gamma_{\text{dir}}\) produce **zero** NS yield of charged hadrons; and

Figure 7.1: Measured trigger yields of TSP (black, light red, and light blue markers). Shaded regions indicate TSP cuts applied to select \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\) triggers.

**Assumption 2,**: that the spectral shape of the \(\pi^{0}\) NS yield of charged hadrons is the same as that of the asymmetric \(\pi^{0}\)/\(\eta\) decays.

First events are selected which satisfy all the event selection criteria listed in tables 8.1 and 8.2 excluding the TSP criterion. From these events, primary tracks are selected which satisfy \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\in(1.2,20)\) GeV/\(c\) as well as all of the track selection criteria listed in 8.4.

Figure 7.2 shows the \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}=p_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}/E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\) distribution of all selected tracks, and figure 7.3 shows the per-trigger yield of selected primary tracks as a function of \(\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk}}\) relative to the trigger azimuth for selected tracks with a \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\in(0.2,0.3)\). The cut on \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\) was to select a clean sample of NS primary tracks: the region \((0.2,0.3)\) strikes a nice balance between providing sufficient statistics without being dominated by too much background. This was determined by considering the signal to background levels in the correlation plots by inspection.

The \(\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk}}\) per-trigger yields were fit with two gaussians, one each for the NS and ASpeaks, and a constant to capture the average background, referred to as the "pedestal," i.e.

\[f\left(\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk}}\right)=a_{0}e^{-\left(\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk }}\right)^{2}/2\sigma_{0}^{2}}+a_{1}e^{-\left(\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk}}-\pi \right)^{2}/2\sigma_{1}^{2}}+a_{2} \tag{7.2}\]

where \(a_{i}\) and \(\sigma_{i}\) are fit parameters. Once fitted, the per-trigger NS yields were extracted by subtracting the pedestal from the per-trigger yield (determined by counting the bin contents) in each bin of \(\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk}}\) and then integrating over the NS region, defined to be \(\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk}}\in(-0.63,0.63)\approx\left(-\frac{\pi}{5},\frac{\pi }{5}\right)\) (the shaded regions in figure 7.3):

\[D_{\text{NS}}^{pp}\left(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}|\star\right)=\int_{-0.63}^{0. 63}\left[\frac{1}{N^{\text{trg}}\left(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}|\star\right)} \frac{dN^{\text{trk}}\left(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}|\star\right)}{d\Delta \varphi^{\text{trk}}}-a_{2}\right]d\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk}} \tag{7.3}\]

where \(D_{\text{NS}}^{pp}\left(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}|\star\right)\) indicates the NS per-trigger yield as a function of \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\) for a given species of trigger (\(\pi^{0}\) or \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\)). Taking the ratio between the NS per-trigger yields of the two species gives

Figure 7.3: \(\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk}}\) of primary tracks selected for the calculation of the \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\) purity integrated over \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\). The solid lines indicate fits consisting of two gaussians and a constant, and the fill areas indicate the NS and AS regions.

\[\mathcal{B}\left(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\right)=\frac{D_{\text{N}}^{pp}\left(E_{ \text{T}}^{\text{trg}}|\gamma_{\text{rich}}\right)}{D_{\text{N}}^{pp}\left(E_{ \text{T}}^{\text{trg}}|\pi^{0}\right)} \tag{7.4}\]

where \(\mathcal{B}\) is the background level of the \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\) sample, the fraction of \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\) triggers which are **not**\(\gamma_{\text{dir}}\). The purity (the fraction of \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\) triggers which **are**\(\gamma_{\text{dir}}\)) is then

\[\mathcal{R}=1-\mathcal{B}. \tag{7.5}\]

### Measurement of \(\mathcal{B}\) and Systematic Uncertainties

Before measuring the value of \(\mathcal{B}\), the second assumption in this calculation, needs to be remarked on here. The \(p_{\text{T}}\) of a decay photon's parent might be different than the \(p_{\text{T}}\) of the measured photon itself, and this may impact the shape of the \(D_{\text{N}}^{pp}\) for background contaminants in the \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\) sample. To test this, a quick Monte-Carlo was used wherein the maximum impact was estimated by assuming that the background in the \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\) sample is due to decay photons exclusively.

First, values of decay photon \(p_{\text{T}}\) (\(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{decay}}\)) were sampled according to the measured \(\gamma_{\text{rich}}\)\(E_{\text{T}}\) distribution. For each \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{decay}}\), the \(p_{\text{T}}\) of the parent \(\pi^{0}\)/\(\eta\) (\(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{parent}}\)) was calculated. For each simulated trigger, a pseudo-event was formed by sampling a certain number of near-side tracks according to the measured \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\) and \(\Delta\varphi^{\text{trk}}\) distributions.

Then the NS per-trigger yields were computed as a function of \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\) twice: once where \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\) was calculated with respect to the decay photon (\(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}/p_{\text{T}}^{\text{decay}}\)), this will be labeled \(D_{\text{N}}^{pp}\left(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}|\gamma_{\text{decay}}\right)\); and once with respect to the parent meson (\(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}/p_{\text{T}}^{\text{parent}}\)), which will be labeled \(D_{\text{N}}^{pp}\left(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}|\pi^{0}\right)\). The ratio was found to agree with unity within 5% agreement for \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\in\left(0.2,0.3\right)\) and within at most 20% otherwise. This indidcates that the impact on \(\mathcal{B}\) due to differences in the shape of \(D_{\text{N}}^{pp}\left(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}|\gamma_{\text{decay}}\right)\) is negligible.

With this confirmed, \(\mathcal{B}\) was calculated as a function of \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\) for the three ranges of used in this analysis. The background level was then averaged over all \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\in(0.1,1)\). The calculated \(\mathcal{B}\) and their average for each of the three ranges of \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\) are shown in figure 7.4. The bin \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}<0.1\) was excluded as backgrounds were too high to reliably extract a NS per-trigger yield. These averages were taken to be _the_ background levels of each range of \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\). Table 7.3 lists the average values of \(\mathcal{B}\) and their uncertainties in \(pp\)-collisions for each of the three bins of \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\). The bin \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trk}}\in(0,0.1)\) was not used due to high backgrounds.

The systematic uncertainty is approximately 10\(\%\) across \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\). This uncertainty is primarily due to the choice of \(z_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\). The choice of integration window in extracting \(D_{\text{NS}}^{pp}\) produces a negligible uncertainty.

