The rationale behind these two parameters is that the only information to characterize jets available at both the particle- and detector-level of the Py6\(\oplus\)Geant framework are the jet 3-momentum and its area. Since the Py6\(\oplus\)Geant framework translates simulated particles into simulated detector responses (which have a finite resolution), the mapping between the set of simulated particles and the set of simulated detector responses is not 1-to-1. Thus the only way to assess how well a reconstructed jet matches a simulated jet is how close the two are in \((\eta,\Delta\varphi)\) space and in \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}\). If a reconstructed jet deviates too much from its simulated counterpart, then it is hard to call the reconstructed jet the "same" jet as the simulated one and thus should be counted as an inefficiency.

In this analysis the parameter \(\Delta R^{\text{match}}\) is set to be \(R_{\text{jet}}\), and the parameter \(Q_{\text{T}}^{\text{match}}\) is set to be \(Q_{\text{T}}^{\text{match}}=(0.5,1.3)\). The tuning of the \(Q_{\text{T}}^{\text{match}}\) parameter will be discussed below. The quantities which these parameters constrain, \(\Delta r^{\text{jet}}\) and \(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}\), are visualized in figure 10.1.

