fit with functions \(P\left(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}|E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\right)\) which approximate the shape of the spectrum that consist of a combination of exponentials and hyperbolic tangents:

\[P\left(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}|E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\right)=\left(\sum_{i=0 }^{n_{\text{exp}}}e^{c_{i}+b_{i}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}}\right)\times\left[ \sum_{j=0}^{n_{\text{tan}}}\text{tanh}\left(\frac{p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}-p_{ \text{T},j}^{0}}{a_{j}}\right)\right] \tag{10.3}\]

where \(a_{i}\), \(b_{i}\), \(c_{j}\) and \(p_{\text{T},j}^{0}\) are parameters, and \(n_{\text{exp}}\) and \(n_{\text{tan}}\) set the number of exponentials and hyperbolic tangents to use for a given range of \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\). The values of \(n_{\text{exp}}\) and \(n_{\text{tan}}\) were arrived at by considering the shape of the prior and trial-and-error. The fit functions are shown in figure 10.2, and the extracted parameters are listed in appendix C. Then \(S\left(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}|E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\right)=\epsilon_{ \text{jet}}\times P\left(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}|E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\right)\) indicates the distribution of unsmeared (particle-level) recoil jet \(p_{\text{T}}\) after applying the jet matching efficiency.

Next the response matrix itself needs to be smoothed. First, projections of \(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}\) for select ranges of \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\) were fit using a double Gaussian function, \(Q\left(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}|p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}},E_{\text{T}}^{\text {trg}}\right)\). The choice of functional form for these fits may seem odd. However, they do reproduce the general shape of the discrete values of \(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}\) as can be seen in figure 10.3. Moreover, in \(pp\) collisions, the number of particles in even \(R_{\text{jet}}=0.5\) jets should be relatively low, especially those at

Figure 10.3: Fit functions used to smooth the \(R_{\text{jet}}=0.5\) response matrices’ \(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}\) projections for 9 - 11 (10.3a), 11 - 15 (10.3b), and 15 - 20 GeV (10.3c) \(\pi^{0}\) triggers. Solid lines indicate the fits.

very low \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\). These very soft jets will be dominated by jets with one or two constituents with roughly equal energy. In these cases, one would expect the matching detector-level jet to have either roughly the same \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) (\(q_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\sim 1\)) as both constituents survived the tracking efficiency, roughly half of the original \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) (\(q_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\sim 0.5\)) as only one of the constituents survived, or to be completely lost as neither constituent survived. These peaks in \(q_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) at 1 and 0.5 will then be smeared out due to the tracking resolution and the inclusion of jets with more than two constituents.

Indeed, this can be seen in the \(q_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) projections at low \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm par}\) in figure 10.3. As the jet \(p_{\rm T}\) increases, these two peaks should become increasingly smeared out as they will become increasingly dominated by jets with substantially more than two constituents, approaching a single peak centered at \(q_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}=1\) with a slowly falling tail on the \(q_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}<1\) side and a sharply falling tail on the \(q_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}>1\) side. This asymmetry is due to the fact that it is substantially

Figure 10.4: Projections of \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm det}\) for a raw matrix (10.4a) versus its smooth counterpart (10.4b) for 9 - 11 GeV \(\pi^{0}\) triggers..

more likely for a jet to lose energy in being reconstructed than it is for it to gain energy. This behavior is also seen in figure 10.3.

The parameters of the functions \(Q\left(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}|p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}},E_{\text{T}}^{\text{ trg}}\right)\) are listed in appendix C. The algorithm by which the \(R_{\text{jet}}=0.5\) response matrices are smoothed is then described in algorithm 6.

```
1:for\(N_{\text{MC}}\) iterations, do
2: Randomly sample a value of \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\) from \(S\left(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}|E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\right)\).
3: Randomly sample a value of \(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}\) from \(Q\left(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}|p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}},E_{\text{T}}^{ \text{trg}}\right)\).
4: Calculate the corresponding detector-level \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{det}}=p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\times q_{\text{T}}^{\text {jet}}\).
5: Add the pair \(\left(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{det}},p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\right)\) to the smoothed response matrix \(\tilde{R}_{ij}\).
6:endfor
7: Normalize \(\tilde{R}_{ij}\) such that \(\int\tilde{R}\left(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{det}},p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\right) dp_{\text{T}}^{\text{det}}=1\).
```

**Algorithm 6** The algorithm for smoothing a response matrix \(R_{ij}\).

The impact of smoothing the response matrix on the matrix itself can be seen in figure 10.4 which shows projections of \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{det}}\) for a raw matrix versus its smoothed counterpart. Then the impact on the unfolded \(R_{\text{jet}}=0.5\) data of smoothing the response matrix can be seen in figure 10.5.

### 10.3 Retraining the Response Matrix

There are instances when the response matrix needs to be retrained on a prior other than the particle-level Py6\(\oplus\)Geant recoil jet spectrum. For instance, both the Py6\(\oplus\)Geant and Py8\(\oplus\)Param samples used to train the response matrix consist solely of \(\pi^{0}\)-triggered dijet events. This could produce a bias in the corrected data, especially in the \(\gamma_{\text{dir}}\)-triggered data.

While this bias is expected to be small due to the robustness of the Bayesian unfolding algorithm and to the fact that (to first order at least) the response matrix should only depend on the jet \(p_{\text{T}}\) and at most weakly on the shape of the training prior. However, this needs to be checked and will be in Section 11.1.2.

When the need arose, the response matrix was be retrained on a different prior via a fast Monte-Carlo simulation. This procedure will be described in terms of retraining the response matrix on a \(\gamma_{\text{dir}}\)-triggered recoil jet spectrum. However, the procedure for retraining the response matrix on something such as a Levy function is identical but with given function substituted for the \(\gamma_{\text{dir}}\)-triggered recoil jet spectrum.

First, the \(R_{\text{jet}}=0.2\) and \(0.5\)\(\gamma_{\text{dir}}\)-triggered recoil jet spectra were generated using PYTHIA 8 for all three ranges of \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\). Let these spectra be labeled \(P_{i}\) where \(i\) runs over

Figure 10.5: 9 - 11 GeV \(\pi^{0}\)-triggered \(R_{\text{jet}}=0.5\) data unfolded using a raw response matrix (black stars) versus using a smoothed response matrix (red circles).

the bins of particle-level jet \(p_{\text{T}}\). Each spectrum was then multiplied by the corresponding jet-matching efficiency; let the resulting spectrum be labeled \(S_{i}=\epsilon_{\text{jet}}\times P\).

Next, values of unsmeared jet \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}\) (\(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\)) were sampled from \(S_{i}\). For each sampled \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\), a smeared jet \(p_{\text{T}}\) (\(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{det}}\)) was sampled from the distribution of detector-level jet \(p_{\text{T}}\) corresponding to the sampled \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\) from the \(\pi^{0}\)-triggered response matrix. Each pair \(\left(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{det}},p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\right)\) was then accumulated in a new response matrix, \(\tilde{R}_{ij}\), and each sampled \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{det}}\) was accumulated in a new detector-level recoil jet spectrum \(\tilde{D}_{i}\). This sampling procedure was repeated a sufficient number of times such that the region defined by \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{par}}\in\left(-1,E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg, max}}\right)\) and \(p_{\text{T}}^{\text{det}}\in\left(-1,E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg, max}}\right)\) (where \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg, max}}\) is the upper limit of the \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\) range) was adequately populated.

Lastly, \(\tilde{R}_{ij}\) and \(\tilde{D}\) were appropriately normalized. Note that for \(R_{\text{jet}}=0.5\) jets, the

