### Accounting for the Trigger Energy Scale and Resolution

As the TES is not one and the TER is nonzero, they must be accounted for when comparing unfolded data against particle-level simulation (e.g. PYTHIA 8 or PYTHIA 6). This is accomplished by first approximating the underlying sampled \(E_{\text{T}}\) distribution of the measured data by "back-smearing" them, i.e. by convoluting the measured \(E_{\text{T}}\) distributions with the reciprocal of the calculated \(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\) distributions. Then, the simulation will be weighted such that its \(E_{\text{T}}\) distributions match the back-smeared data.

In order to quantify the TES and TER, the central peak of \(q_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\) distribution, \(Q_{\text{T}}^{\text{trg}}\) is

