In order to compare the fully-corrected data to simulation, the _simulated_ recoil jet distributions are reweighted such that the \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trg}\) distributions of their correlated triggers match \(\tilde{E}_{\rm T}^{\rm trg}\). There are two simulation samples which will be compared against the fully corrected data: a sample of "out-of-the-box" PYTHIA 8.185 - i.e. using the Monash Tune described in chapter 9.2 - and a sample of PYTHIA 6.42 tuned to STAR data. These two samples will be labeled Py8 and Py6\(\star\) respectively. The trigger \(E_{\rm T}\) distribution (\(E_{\rm T}^{\rm Py8}\)) extends down to 9 GeV. While in Py6\(\star\), the trigger \(E_{\rm T}\) distribution (\(E_{\rm T}^{\rm Py6\star}\)) extends down to 7 GeV(for \(\gamma\) triggers) and 6 GeV (for \(\pi^{0}\) triggers).

In calculating the weights to be applied to Py8 and Py6\(\star\), no cut on \(E_{\text{T}}\) will be applied to either simulation. First, the distributions \(\tilde{E}_{\text{TF}}^{\text{meas}}\), \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{Py8}}\), and \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{Py6}\star}\) are normalized to unity. Then the weights are given by taking the ratio of \(\tilde{E}_{\text{TF,D}}^{\text{meas}}\) and \(E_{\text{T}}^{\text{Py8,Py6}\star}\):

